            are simply elements of the gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝔤
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
            for a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+Lie+algebra"} matrix Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} physics
            is called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em IKKT matrix model
          %p
            Alternatively this model can be motivated from a certain regularization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#IKKT96"} IKKT 96
          %p
            It has been argued that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔤
                  %mo =
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{g} = \mathfrak{su}(N)
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+Lie+algebra"} special unitary Lie algebra
            and in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} type IIB string theory
            (which would be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            %em IIB matrix model
            (in contrast to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BFSS+matrix+model"} BFSS matrix model
            in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
          %p
            Several authors have explored the possibility to lift the derivation of the IKKT model from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M2-brane"} M2-brane
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/membrane+matrix+model"} membrane matrix model
            for more on this.
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#KimNishimuraTsuchiya12"}> Kim-Nishimura-Tsuchiya 12
            ) it is claimed that computer simulation of the IKKT matrix model, regarded as non-perturbative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} type IIB string theory
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brandenberger-Vafa+mechanism"} Brandenberger-Vafa mechanism
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %div
            %p
              %strong>
